//? Title: Minimum enclosing circles
//? Subtitle: Actual boundary rebuilds, tolerance and the original normal cloud
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Find a small circle enclosing a finite planar point set. Its exact geometric
optimum is determined by at most three boundary points, which permits
incremental boundary rebuilding. We show actual containment decisions on a
triangle, retain the original eighty-point normal cloud, and distinguish
tolerance-aware numerical enclosure from an exact support or minimality
certificate.
)

\toc

> Why at most three points suffice

An exact smallest enclosing circle is unique: if two distinct centers gave the
same minimal radius, their midpoint would cover all points with a smaller radius
by the squared-distance identity. Its essential boundary set has either two
diametrically opposite points or three points defining a circumcircle; one point
suffices for a singleton. More boundary points may lie on the same circle
without being essential.

Two boundary points a,b give center(a+b)/2 and radius half their distance. For
three noncollinear points, subtracting the equations for their squared distances
gives a two-by-two linear system for the circumcenter. Translated long-double
arithmetic avoids unnecessary large global-coordinate squares; exact orientation
identifies genuinely collinear triples, which use the farthest-pair diameter.
Unrepresentable geometry throws rather than returning an infinite circle.

The producer uses a fixed-seed shuffle and three nested loops. An outer
violation resets to that point; a preceding violating point makes a diameter;
another violation makes a three-point circle. This boundary-set strategy
motivates the method, but the explicit implementation has O(n cubed) worst-case
containment tests. We make no expected-linear claim for this particular code.

> A small right triangle with actual rebuilds

The points(0,0),(4,0),(0,3) have exact optimum center(2,1.5) and radius2.5, the
hypotenuse diameter. The trace records actual tests in shuffled order and each
immediate circle rebuild. Its diagram follows every failed test; contained tests
remain in the table but do not change the circle.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/circle_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::geometry::min_enclosing_circle_trace({{0, 0}, {4, 0}, {0, 3}});
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("initial", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual singleton\nradius {}", t.initial.radius));
    std::string previous = "initial";
    std::size_t change = 0;
    std::print("| depth | original point ID | contained | radius before | radius immediately after |\n| " "---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &e : t.events) {
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", e.depth, e.input_index,
                   e.contained, e.before.radius, e.after.radius);
        if (!e.contained) {
            auto id = std::to_string(++change);
            d.node(id, {5.0 * change, 0},
                   std::format("Actual depth {} rebuild\nradius {}", e.depth,
                               e.after.radius)).edge(previous, id);
            previous = id;
        }
    }
    std::print("\nFinal center: ({},{}), radius: {}.\n", t.result.center.x,
               t.result.center.y, t.result.radius);
    d.save_svg("build/circle_tiny.svg");
}
```
