> Translation, collinearity and scale-sensitive tolerance

Collinear points0,2,4 on a horizontal line have midpoint2 and radius2.
Translating them by a large representable offset should preserve the radius. The
finite controls below retain duplicates and also show a tiny triangle whose
deviations fall below the absolute part of the containment tolerance. A
zero-radius numerical answer for such tiny data can satisfy the API while
differing from the exact geometric optimum. The same tolerance prevents
interpreting the result as a scale-invariant exact certificate.

The trace owns original points, actual shuffled points, their original IDs and
actual containment/rebuild events. Its quota counts three units per input and
one per event, default1000000; zero disables. Failure returns no partial
history. Ordinary execution has O(n) copied storage; capture adds one record per
actual test, potentially cubic. Empty or nonfinite input throws; duplicates,
singletons and collinearity are accepted.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/circle_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D>> cases{
        {{0, 0}, {2, 0}, {4, 0}, {2, 0}},
        {{1e12, 1e12}, {1e12 + 2, 1e12}, {1e12 + 4, 1e12}},
        {{0, 0}, {2e-12, 0}, {1e-12, 2e-12}}, {{3, 4}}
    };
    std::vector<std::string> labels{"collinear", "translated", "tiny triangle",
                                    "singleton"};
    std::vector<double> radii;
    std::print("| case | radius | outside tolerance | near boundary |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < cases.size(); ++i) {
        auto c = dm::geometry::min_enclosing_circle(cases[i]);
        auto a = dm::geometry::enclosing_circle_check(c, cases[i]);
        radii.push_back(c.radius);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", labels[i], c.radius, a.outside,
                   a.near_boundary);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(radii, labels, "returned radius");
    p.title("Numerical controls: translation, degeneracy and tolerance").x_label("input case").y_label("returned radius").save_svg("build/circle_controls.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| case | radius | outside tolerance | near boundary |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| collinear | 2 | 0 | 2 |
| translated | 2 | 0 | 2 |
| tiny triangle | 0 | 0 | 3 |
| singleton | 0 | 0 | 1 |
\image(figures/circle_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Returned radii for explicit geometry and tolerance controls.)
\alttext(Ordinary and translated collinear controls have radius2; tiny geometry demonstrates the absolute containment tolerance.)

> Conclusion and references

Actual rebuild records connect the boundary-set idea to the returned triangle
and cloud circles. Radial residuals and scale controls expose the numerical
contract beyond what the plotted outline can establish. The explicit cubic
worst-case loop bound, finite geometry guards and tolerance limit the claims
made for this implementation.

[Welzl, Smallest enclosing disks (balls and ellipsoids) (1991)|https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0038202]
gives the boundary-set framework. This report executes the native nested-loop
variant rather than reproducing the recursive pseudocode of the original source
report.

> Verification

The ten-report batch passes95 CTest cases,31 directly instrumented ASan/UBSan
cases,83 compiled and executed public examples,363/363 scoped documentation
definitions and ten representative hovers. Independent Python integer
arithmetic, MT19937-64/variation replay, graph sets, analytic interpolation,
circle boundary enumeration and actual-sample integration reconstruction agree
on915459 captured records. All six original RMS summaries are separately checked
using96 native MISER estimates and1600000 independently reproduced
plain-comparator points. Frozen valid-input comparisons include the original
seeded solver paths and full MOEA/D fronts. Ten checkout headers and ten
identically installed development headers compile independently, and an
installed-header native consumer links and runs. Each report has four executed
complete programs, four reviewed native figures and both HTML/Markdown exports
with computed-result and image-byte checks. This is a scoped native API audit,
not an installation claim for every language binding. Receipts:
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-234-243/`.
